=== why the push probe must attempt a write, 2026-09-06T18:16:00Z ===

--- 1. ghcr.io token endpoint, WITH a credential, asking for push on our package ---
GET /token?scope=repository:link-foundation/box:pull,push -> HTTP 200
body: {"token": "<56-char opaque string>"}
base64-decode(token) == the credential we sent: True
=> the endpoint echoed the credential back. It verified nothing: not the
   credential, and not the "push" scope that was asked for.

--- 2. the write itself, with that very token ---
POST /v2/link-foundation/box/blobs/uploads/ -> HTTP 403
{"errors":[{"code":"DENIED","message":"permission_denied: The token provided does not match expected scopes."}]}

=> HTTP 200 from the token endpoint, HTTP 403 from the registry. A preflight
   built on the token endpoint alone passes for a credential that cannot push.

--- 3. docker.io, anonymous, asking for push on konard/box ---
GET /token?scope=repository:konard/box:pull,push -> HTTP 200
granted access claim: [{"actions": ["pull"], "name": "konard/box", "parameters": {"pull_limit": "100", "pull_limit_interval": "21600", "registry_region": "us-east-1"}, "type": "repository"}]
=> HTTP 200 again, for a caller with no credential at all. Docker Hub grants
   what it is willing to grant ("pull") rather than refusing the request, so
   the status code says nothing about write access here either.

--- 4. docker.io token endpoint with a wrong credential (the one case a status code does answer) ---
HTTP 401 {"details":"incorrect username or password"}

